S|               EXT. EMPTY CAMP - NIGHT                                              
O|
N|               Moonlight shines down on the clearing, the ashes of the              
N|               firepit, the snow-covered lean-to. If there were corpses on          
N|               the ground before, they're gone now.                                 
O|
N|               The three riders enter the camp. Their horses seem spooked,          
N|               as if they smell a predator nearby.                                  
O|
C|                                   SER WAYMAR                                       
U|                         Your dead men seem to have moved                           
U|                         camp.                                                      
N|               Will looks around, confused. He knows what he saw.                   
O|
C|                                   WILL                                             
U|                         They were here ...                                         
O|
N|               Spotting something shimmering on the ground near the firepit,        
N|               he walks his horse closer, dismounts and looks down.                 
O|
N|               Lying at his feet is the hilt of a steel longsword. The blade        
N|               has been shattered into a thousand shards.                           
N|               Will stares at the shattered sword. He knows what this means;        
N|               the dread on his face is unmistakable.                               
O|
N|               From his horse, Gared looks at the ground. The indentations          
N|               where the bodies once lay are still visible-- as are the             
N|               faint but unmistakable footprints leading away from them.            
O|
C|                                   GARED                                            
U|                         We have to move. Now.                                      
O|
N|               He is interrupted by a neigh. Will's horse, riderless and            
N|               panicked, bolts from the camp site.                                  
O|                                             TV Calling - For educational purposes only
O|
O|
O|                                                                      3.            
O|
N|               Ser Waymar's horse rears back on its hind legs, throwing its         
N|               rider to the ground before galloping after the first horse.          
N|               Gared struggles to keep his own horse under control. Ser             
N|               Waymar stands unsteadily, brushing the snow from his cloak.          
O|
C|                                   WILL                                             
P|                             (terrified)                                            
U|                         Gods ...                                                   
O|
N|               He's staring into the darkness at the edge of the clearing.          
N|               Ser Waymar turns to see what the young tracker sees: a shadow        
N|               emerging from the forest.                                            
N|               A figure steps into the moonlight, tall and gaunt, with flesh        
N|               pale as milk. It slides toward the rangers on silent feet.           
O|
N|               Its armor appears to be carved from ice. Its sword is                
N|               translucent, a shard of crystal so thin it almost seems to           
N|               vanish when seen edge-on.                                            
O|
N|               Ser Waymar's voice cracks like a boy's:                              
O|
C|                                   SER WAYMAR                                       
U|                         Stay where you are!                                        
O|
N|               The OTHER keeps coming. Ser Waymar draws his sword with              
N|               trembling hands. Will, standing near the fire pit, and Gared,        
N|               still on horseback, draw their own swords.                           
N,M,G|               The Other halts. For the first time we see its eyes, bluer           
N|               than any human eyes.                                                 
O|
N|               They emerge silently from the shadows, on all sides of the           
N|               clearing. Five of them ... six ... seven ... their strange           
N|               swords shimmering in the moonlight.                                  
O|
N|               Gared can no longer control his panicked horse; it bolts from        
N|               the clearing, ignoring its rider's commands.                         
N|               The Others watch Gared flee. They turn back to Ser Waymar and        
N|               Will and begin to advance on the young men.                          
O|
N|               As the circle closes, the Others speak to each other in a            
N|               language we've never heard, with voices like cracking ice.           
O|
N|               Waymar and Will stand together, class distinctions forgotten,        
N|               two boys about to die. They steady their sword hands and             
N|               mutter quick prayers as the Others descend upon them.                
O|
M|               CREDIT SEQUENCE                                                      
O|
N,G|               CLOSE on a pair of ancient, gnarled hands writing a message          
O|                                             TV Calling - For educational purposes only
N|               on a small parchment scroll.                                         
O|
O|
O|                                                                      4.            
O|
N,M|               The old man (we never see his face) tightly rolls the scroll,        
N|               binds it with a black ribbon, and ties it with a leather             
N|               strip to the leg of a BLACK RAVEN.                                   
N|               The old man lifts the raven off his desk; it flaps its dark          
N|               wings and flies out of the open chamber window.                      
O|
N|               The raven flies away from Castle Black, a large and ancient          
N|               fortress dwarfed by what lies behind it: the Wall. Older than        
N|               history, this is the 800-foot-high barrier of ice and stone          
N|               that guards the northern edge of the Seven Kingdoms.                 
O|
N|               As the raven gains altitude, the landscape below it                  
N|               TRANSFORMS into a map of Westeros. The Wall is revealed to           
N|               cross the entire continent, a boundary between the Haunted           
N|               Forest to the north and the civilized lands to the south.            
N|               The raven flies south over the map, on which the cities,             
N|               '.regions and features of the land are named: Winterfell, the        
N|               Kingsroad, Moat Cailin, the Riverlands, the Vale of Arryn.           
O|
N|               Occasionally the bird dips down, and the map resolves back           
N|               in reality for just long enough to give us a view of some          
N|               points of interest: Winterfell's old stone towers, full of           
N|               cold beauty. The foreboding Eyrie castle high atop the Vale          
N|               of Arryn, a feat of montane architecture that would have been        
N|               impossible for medieval engineers.                                   
N|               When the raven reaches King's Landing, the map resolves back         
N|               int. reality as the bird drops down into the dirty sprawl of         
N|               the capital.                                                         
O|
N|               The raven flies through the open gates of the Red Keep, a            
N|               massive compound with walls the color of fresh-spilt blood.          
N|               The bird flies through an open window into the throne room,          
N|               to land on the Iron Throne itself-- a throne built from the          
N|               hammered swords of a thousand defeated enemies.                      
O|
N|               The raven pecks at its wings, cleaning itself after the long         
N|               journey, alone in the empty throne room.                             
M|               END CREDIT SEQUENCE                                                  
O|
S|               EXT. HILLTOP - DAY                                                   
O|
N,I|               LORD EDDARD "NED" STARK (40) sits on his motionless horse,           
N,I|               his long brown hair stirring in the wind. His closely-trimmed        
N|               beard is shot with white. He has spent half his life training        
N|               for war and the other half waging it, and his face conveys           
N|               both authority and a haunted sadness.                                
O|
N|               He is accompanied by a dozen members of the HOUSEHOLD GUARD,         
N|               all on horseback. Over their heads flaps the Stark banner: a         
O|                                             TV Calling - For educational purposes only
N|               gray direwolf racing across an ice-white field.                      
O|
O|
O|                                                                      5.            
O|
N|               South of the Wall the grass is still green and the sun is            
N|               shining, but the air is cold enough to cloud the breath of           
N|               four guardsmen as they escort Gared toward the stump of a            
N|               massive oak tree.                                                    
N|               Weeks have passed since we saw the ranger but he seems to            
N|               have aged years, his eyes bloodshot and weary, his face              
N|               haggard, his clothes filthy and torn.                                
O|
N,I|               Ned's sons, JON SNOW (17), ROBB STARK (17) and BRAN STARK (8)        
N|               sit on their own horses (a small pony in Bran's case).               
O|
N|               Jon is slender, darker than his half-brothers, his eyes black        
N|               and watchful.                                                        
N|               Robb is big and broad, with fair skin and reddish-brown hair.        
O|
N|               Bran sits very still, watching the doomed man. He has never          
N|               seen an execution before. The prospect of it terrifies him,          
N|               but he tries hard to imitate his father's solemn expression.         
O|
N|               The Guardsmen force Gared's head onto the stump. Ned                 
N|               dismounts.                                                           
O|
